## 6\. AI assistant and AI-generated content

AI must improve understanding without introducing incorrect explanations or misleading exam material.

### Required functionality

- Answer questions using available transcript passages and source references.
- Clearly distinguish transcript-supported statements from general explanations.
- Allow the student to select a passage, formula, or timestamp before asking a question.
- Explain derivations step by step and render mathematical expressions correctly.
- Generate alternative examples and progressively harder practice questions.
- Support follow-up questions with conversation context.
- Save useful explanations to the AI Doubt Notebook.
- Generate lecture summaries at multiple levels of detail.
- Validate generated questions and answer keys before scored use.
- Detect and flag uncertain or ambiguous questions.
- Cache reusable summaries where appropriate.
- Apply usage limits and handle timeouts, model failures, and unavailable transcripts.
- Keep private API credentials on the server.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Add suggested prompts such as “Explain simply,” “Show derivation,” “Give a GATE-style example,” and “Explain my mistake.”
- Display the source timestamp or transcript passage when an answer is grounded in the lecture.
- Add Copy, Save to Notes, Ask Follow-up, and Report Incorrect Answer actions.
- Use clear mathematical formatting and collapsible step-by-step solutions.
- Distinguish generated practice questions from verified PYQs with persistent labels.
- Provide loading, retry, partial-result, and error states.
- Avoid interrupting a video with automatic AI-generated pop-ups.

Acceptance criterion: The assistant must not invent lecture content or present unverified AI-generated questions as official GATE questions.

## 7\. Notes creator and Formula Vault

### Required functionality

- Generate notes from accessible transcripts, summaries, bookmarks, and saved explanations.
- Support editing, rearranging, annotations, and hierarchical topic organization.
- Link notes to videos, timestamps, syllabus topics, and relevant questions.
- Maintain formula collections with conditions, assumptions, derivations, and examples.
- Mark content as Important, Difficult, Frequently Tested, or Needs Revision.
- Support full-text search, tags, PDF export, and printing.
- Preserve note versions or provide undo for accidental edits.
- Enable offline access to selected notes where feasible.
- Render equations correctly in both the editor and exported PDFs.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Use a readable editor with a clear heading hierarchy and consistent spacing.
- Add a collapsible subject/topic navigation tree.
- Provide a formula-focused reading mode with adjustable text size.
- Show source references beside AI-generated content.
- Display clear autosave and synchronization status.
- Offer one-click insertion of saved formulas and AI explanations.
- Add a compact revision view without editing controls or unnecessary navigation.

Acceptance criterion: A student should be able to retrieve a specific formula, mistake, or lecture explanation in seconds rather than reopening an entire lecture.

## 8\. Practice module and question bank

### Required functionality

- Support MCQ, MSQ, NAT, conceptual, formula-based, and application-oriented questions.
- Filter questions by paper, subject, topic, subtopic, year, difficulty, and question type.
- Distinguish verified PYQs, model questions, and AI-generated questions.
- Track attempted, correct, incorrect, skipped, and bookmarked questions.
- Provide configurable question count, difficulty, duration, and feedback mode.
- Generate tests based on weak concepts and previous mistakes.
- Track repeat attempts without confusing them with first-attempt performance.
- Build custom question collections and revision sets.
- Include detailed explanations and relevant source links.
- Offer practice modes with immediate feedback and exam modes with deferred feedback.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Make filters easy to combine and reset.
- Keep the question, diagrams, formulas, answer choices, and navigation visually distinct.
- Show question status without depending on color alone.
- Provide keyboard navigation and accessible controls.
- Allow students to flag an incorrect question or explanation.
- Make the next action after submission explicit: Review mistakes, Retry, Revise concept, or Continue.

### Essential performance metrics

Track first-attempt accuracy separately from repeat-attempt accuracy. Also track time per question, confidence where collected, and the reasons for incorrect answers.

Acceptance criterion: Every incorrect response should be linked to a relevant concept and have a clear route into targeted revision.

## 9\. Mock examination simulator

The mock exam interface should be treated as a reliability-critical feature.

### Required functionality

- Support full papers, sectional tests, subject tests, topic tests, and custom sets.
- Use the applicable official syllabus, marking rules, and examination structure when verified.
- Support answer saving, mark for review, question palettes, navigation, and submission confirmation.
- Autosave answers and recover interrupted sessions.
- Calculate marks, accuracy, time spent, attempted questions, and unanswered questions correctly.
- Support a strict Exam Mode, explanatory Practice Mode, and post-exam Analysis Mode.
- Allow partial practice sessions without forcing the student to finish a full paper.
- Preserve the exact question set and attempt state for resumed exams.
- Provide a review of incorrect, skipped, guessed, and time-consuming questions.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Keep the timer, question number, marks, and answer controls visible without obscuring the question.
- Use distinct but accessible states for Answered, Unanswered, Marked for Review, and Answered and Marked for Review.
- Include a navigable question palette and clear section controls.
- Require confirmation before final submission.
- Provide a review screen listing unanswered questions before submission.
- Prevent unrelated notifications, animations, and automatic plan updates from interrupting the exam.
- Make network interruption and recovery states obvious.

Acceptance criterion: A student must never lose a submitted answer because of an accidental refresh, navigation event, or temporary network failure.

## 10\. Revision engine and Error Notebook

### Required functionality

- Automatically create revision items from mistakes, difficult PYQs, forgotten formulas, and weak topics.
- Use spaced repetition with adjustable review intervals.
- Let users rate recall confidence and understanding.
- Schedule follow-up questions to verify actual retention.
- Record the original question, submitted answer, correct answer, root cause, corrective concept, and subsequent results.
- Reduce revision priority only when supported by consistent evidence.
- Support flashcards, formula drills, timed revision, and short diagnostic quizzes.
- Provide a final revision mode that consolidates high-priority formulas and repeated mistakes.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Separate the daily revision queue from the complete mistake archive.
- Show why each item is due and estimate the time needed.
- Provide quick review actions without forcing the student through multiple pages.
- Make “I understand” different from “I can solve questions correctly.”
- Offer batch revision sessions and a clear session completion summary.

Acceptance criterion: Revision priority should depend on demonstrated retention and repeated errors, not simply the age of a saved note.

## 11\. Analytics and exam-readiness tracking

### Required functionality

- Track study hours, focused time, syllabus coverage, PYQ completion, and revision completion.
- Measure subject and topic mastery using multiple attempts over time.
- Track first-attempt accuracy, solving speed, repeated errors, and retention.
- Compare mock scores across attempts using comparable test conditions.
- Identify subjects that are improving and those that need intervention.
- Generate weekly performance reviews.
- Estimate readiness using transparent factors rather than a single arbitrary score.
- Show whether the student is on schedule, ahead, or behind.
- Provide specific recommendations tied to the underlying evidence.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Use a main overview with a few meaningful metrics.
- Add detailed pages for Subject Mastery, Mock Performance, Time Management, and Syllabus Coverage.
- Show trends over time rather than isolated scores.
- Allow charts to be filtered by subject, topic, date range, and test type.
- Explain every readiness estimate and show the factors contributing to it.
- Pair each important chart with a recommended action.

For example, instead of displaying only “Algorithms mastery: 48%,” show:

> Your recent accuracy on graph algorithms is low, and solving time is increasing. Review the prerequisite concepts and complete a short diagnostic test.

Acceptance criterion: Every important analytics page should answer both “What is happening?” and “What should I do about it?”

## 12\. Focus mode and productivity tools

### Required functionality

- Provide configurable Pomodoro sessions and custom study intervals.
- Track focused work separately from passive video playback.
- Let students define a session objective and completion criteria.
- Include short breaks and optional longer breaks.
- Provide a quick-capture feature for doubts and reminders.
- Record session outcomes and blockers.
- Preserve the current session if the page reloads or the browser closes unexpectedly.
- Avoid disrupting active study sessions with automatic schedule changes.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Hide the global sidebar, secondary widgets, and nonessential notifications.
- Keep the current task, timer, learning content, and quick-capture tool visible.
- Provide simple pause, resume, finish, and break controls.
- Make the interface usable with keyboard shortcuts.
- Support reduced motion and configurable reminders.
- Provide a brief session-end review instead of a large celebratory screen.

Acceptance criterion: Entering focus mode should visibly reduce distractions, and leaving it should preserve the student's work and session record.

## 13\. Notifications and gamification

These features should improve consistency without making the app noisy or punitive.

### Required functionality

- Allow users to configure quiet hours, reminder channels, frequency, and intensity.
- Prioritize urgent revision and scheduled tests.
- Consolidate less urgent reminders.
- Allow direct navigation from a reminder to the relevant task.
- Reward meaningful learning, improvement, and revision consistency.
- Recognize personal bests and milestones.
- Allow missed-day recovery without punishing students.
- Provide options to disable celebratory effects and motivational messages.
- Avoid awarding substantial rewards for idle time or repeated low-value actions.

### Required UI/UX changes

- Use a notification center with priority, date, and action.
- Provide a clear “Mark as read” and “Mute” control.
- Keep achievement animations brief and optional.
- Avoid making streaks the main measure of preparation success.
- Celebrate improved accuracy, retained concepts, and completion of difficult work—not just hours logged.

Acceptance criterion: Notifications and rewards should encourage the next useful learning action rather than increase app usage for its own sake.

